		{
			"id": "eDzsU2cznCZ4M6Nxb",
			"description": "Power Klaw",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Ork answer to the power fist and the single most iconic Nob and Meganob weapon: a huge armoured gauntlet crackling with an unstable energy field, consistently shown across Codex: Orks background and Black Library Ork fiction crushing Space Marines, vehicle hulls and even Dreadnoughts one-handed - the framework's own anchor table cites it by name as \"like a power fist but cruder.\"\n\nDesign: Matches the framework's Ork anchor exactly (\"Power klaw | Power-field weapon: thr+8d(10) cr, like a power fist but cruder\"), built with the same first-principles logic as the Imperium Power Fist: the field's own servos generate most of the force, so the bonus is a large flat die addition to the wielder's own thrust (thr+8d) rather than a multiplier, and it shares the project's Armor Divisor (10) power-field rule. Checked by exact dice distribution at a mega-armoured Meganob's effective thrust (around thr 3d+1, giving thr+8d = 11d+1): avg 39.5, penetrating every personal armour on the table essentially always, including a Terminator (100%) - matching the anchor's own \"like a power fist ... kills Terminators\" reputation and the fiction's constant depiction of power klaws cracking open Dreadnoughts and Terminator squads alike. Even at a bare Boy's own strength (thr 2d, thr+8d=10d, avg 35) it still clears every personal armour at or above 99.7%, since the field itself - \"cruder\" than the Imperium version, per the anchor, but not meaningfully weaker in its actual armour- divisor effect - is doing almost all of the penetrating work regardless of who swings it. \"Cruder\" is modelled instead in cost, reliability and the entry's own tone rather than a lower divisor, since dropping the divisor below (10) would break the anchor's own explicit \"like a power fist\" equivalence. Handling: 45 lb, matching the Imperium Power Fist's own weight class for a similarly huge servo-driven gauntlet; min ST 22 (a Boy's own strength, though in practice almost always a Nob or Meganob wears one, per the framework's \"built for Ork hands\" convention). Reach C, parry -2 and the no-Feint note directly mirror the Imperium Power Fist's own handling rules, since both weapons share the same \"huge servo-fist, slow to reposition\" fighting style.",
			"tags": [
				"Melee Weapon",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"tech_level": "7",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "9000",
			"base_weight": "45 lb",
